WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.220
A rod cell detects a single photon by turning an electrical current down.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.800
How can destroying a messenger carry a signal?

3
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.000
Every cascade you met before made a messenger appear.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.180
Start with a rod resting in darkness.

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:25.400
In darkness, guanylyl cyclase keeps making cGMP, while its breakdown runs only

6
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.820
slowly. So cGMP stays high.

7
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.340
This messenger binds directly to cation channels, holding some of them open with no

8
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.540
kinase in between.

9
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.740
Sodium and calcium stream inward.

10
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.720
That steady dark current leaves the rod partly depolarized, releasing glutamate

11
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.720
nonstop.

12
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:53.043
Inside the disc membrane sits rhodopsin, a receptor holding bent retinal already

13
00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:54.203
inside its pocket.

14
00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:57.423
That bent shape locks rhodopsin off.

15
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:03.403
When a photon arrives, it straightens retinal, and the protein around it is pushed

16
00:01:03.403 --> 00:01:09.463
into a new shape. Rhodopsin is now active, and every later step runs without light.

17
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:16.617
Active rhodopsin switches on many copies of transducin, swapping GDP for GTP.

18
00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:24.117
Activated transducin lifts an inhibitory cap off the phosphodiesterase PDE6.

19
00:01:25.357 --> 00:01:30.157
The freed enzyme rapidly breaks cGMP into inactive GMP.

20
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.997
As messenger levels plunge, channels snap shut.

21
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:40.390
With channels closed, positive inflow shrinks, the membrane hyperpolarizes, and

22
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.630
glutamate release drops.

23
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.050
The next neuron reads that drop as light.

24
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:52.630
To reset, phosphorylation and arrestin silence rhodopsin, while GTP hydrolysis turns

25
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:58.070
transducin off. Still, the rod must refill its depleted messenger pool.

26
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:04.393
While channels stood open in darkness, calcium entered steadily and an exchanger

27
00:02:04.393 --> 00:02:05.473
pumped it back out.

28
00:02:06.813 --> 00:02:11.413
Light closed the channels, so entry stopped, pumping continued, and calcium fell.

29
00:02:12.813 --> 00:02:17.293
Low calcium switches on helper proteins that speed up guanylyl cyclase.

30
00:02:18.553 --> 00:02:19.973
Now keep a dim light shining.

31
00:02:21.293 --> 00:02:22.493
Will the channels stay shut?

32
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:35.720
No. The faster cyclase refills the messenger partway, so some channels reopen under

33
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:41.100
the light. This negative feedback lets the rod adapt to background light.

34
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.380
With some channels open again, the rod has current left to shut off, so an extra

35
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.580
flash still registers.

36
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.670
Now compare the eye with the nose.

37
00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:58.570
Each olfactory neuron expresses a single odorant receptor from a very large family.

38
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:05.710
When an odorant binds, a G protein activates adenylyl cyclase, so cAMP rises.

39
00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:12.730
The rising messenger opens a related cation channel, and the neuron depolarizes and

40
00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:13.730
fires.

41
00:03:14.283 --> 00:03:20.383
Both senses rely on closely related cyclic-nucleotide channels, yet wire them in

42
00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:21.463
opposite directions.

43
00:03:22.943 --> 00:03:25.763
Light drives cGMP down from its dark baseline.

44
00:03:27.023 --> 00:03:30.183
An odorant drives the nose's messenger up from rest.

45
00:03:31.603 --> 00:03:36.363
Keep this rule. A signal is a change from baseline, up or down.
